<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishnaraja Wodeyar III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadiyar-dynasty">Wadiyar dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krishnaraja Wodeyar III was maharaja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1799 to 1868. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>'s defeat in 1799 the British placed him on the throne as a five-year-old, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purnaiah">Purnaiah</a> governing. Following a peasant revolt in 1831 the British took over the administration, and he spent the rest of his reign as a patron of music, literature and the arts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-iii.jpg" alt="Krishnaraja Wodeyar III" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krishnaraja_Wadiyar_III,_Maharaja_of_Mysore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> on 14 July 1794, the son of Maharaja Chamaraja Wadiyar IX and his first wife, Maharani Kempananjammani Devi. His adoptive grandmother, Maharani Lakshmi Devi, oversaw his education and upbringing. Through the years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and Tipu she had sent feelers to the British, hoping to see the kingdom returned to the Wadiyars, and she informed them of Tipu's treaty with the French. After Tipu's death in 1799 she negotiated the handing over of the throne.</p><p>The boy was installed on 30 June 1799 in a pavilion built near the Lakshmiramana Swamy temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, under her regency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Arthur Wellesley</a> led Lakshmi Devi to the ceremony, and she arranged for Tipu's adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purnaiah">Purnaiah</a> to stay on as Dewan, loyal to the king until he came of age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At sixteen, in early 1810, he was no longer counted a minor, and after discussion with Arthur Cole, the British Resident, the government passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purnaiah">Purnaiah</a> to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>. His grandmother died that year and Purnaiah in 1812. Relations with the British were cordial until the 1820s. In 1825 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-munro">Thomas Munro</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, found no substance in Cole's charges of financial impropriety, but the Nagar revolt changed matters. In 1831, citing maladministration, the British set up the Mysore Commission; Mark Cubbon headed it from 1834 to 1861. The Maharaja died on 27 March 1868 and was succeeded by his grandson and adopted son, Chamarajendra Wadiyar X.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> was himself a writer, credited with more than 50 works, among them Sritattvanidhi, Saugandhika Parinaya and Surya Chandra Vamsavali, which retells a hundred episodes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and the deeds of the dynasty's founders. He knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, Tamil, English, Telugu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, played the veena, and is credited with reviving the game of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganjifa">Ganjifa</a>. His court helped shape modern Kannada prose and the survival of Yakshagana. Basavappa Shastri translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/othello">Othello</a> into Kannada as Shurasena Charita, and the royal workshop produced murals and illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> and the Ramayana.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Krishnaraja Wodeyar III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Company annexed much of his territory, giving portions to Hyderabad, and restored the Wodeyar dynasty in the person of the four-year-old <b>Krishnaraja Wodeyar III</b>, with Purnaiah, Tipu's former minister, as diwan, over a reduced princely state under British protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Tipu Sultan was killed at Seringapatam in 1799, the British restored the Hindu Wodeyar dynasty, which Hyder Ali had sidelined, and placed the five-year-old <b>Krishnaraja Wodeyar III</b> on the throne under the diwan Purnaiah.</blockquote><a href="/state/mysore-state">Mysore State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.42,76.7&amp;year=1842" aria-label="Srirangapatna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Krishnaraja Wodeyar III"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3899 3946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3899 3946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.42,76.7&amp;year=1842">What was here in 1842? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
